1
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:17,560
The goal here is simple, explain what a

2
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:19,000
derivative is.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,040
The thing is though, there's some

4
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:21,880
subtlety to this topic and a lot of

5
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:23,720
potential for paradoxes if you're not

6
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:26,160
careful. So kind of a secondary goal is

7
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:27,880
that you have an appreciation for what

8
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:29,880
those paradoxes are and how to avoid

9
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:31,200
them.

10
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:32,759
You see, it's common for people to say

11
00:00:32,759 --> 00:00:34,240
that the derivative measures an

12
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:36,840
instantaneous rate of change. But when

13
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,440
you think about it, that phrase is

14
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:40,640
actually an oxymoron. Change is

15
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:42,240
something that happens between separate

16
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:44,320
points in time and when you blind

17
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:45,880
yourself to all but just a single

18
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:48,040
instant, there's not really any room for

19
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:49,440
change.

20
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:50,680
You'll see what I mean more as we get

21
00:00:50,680 --> 00:00:52,680
into it, but when you appreciate that a

22
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:54,960
phrase like instantaneous rate of change

23
00:00:54,960 --> 00:00:56,960
is actually nonsense, I think it makes

24
00:00:56,960 --> 00:00:58,360
you appreciate just how clever the

25
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:00,600
fathers of calculus were in capturing

26
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,280
the idea that that phrase is meant to

27
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,320
evoke, but with a perfectly sensible

28
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:07,440
piece of math, the derivative.

29
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:09,040
As our central example, I want you to

30
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:10,960
imagine a car that starts at some point

31
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:13,600
A, speeds up, and then slows down to a

32
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:16,720
stop at some point B 100 m away. And

33
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:18,040
let's say it all happens over the course

34
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:20,520
of 10 seconds.

35
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:22,400
That's the setup to have in mind as we

36
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:24,520
lay out what the derivative is.

37
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:26,520
We could graph this motion, letting the

38
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,520
vertical axis represent the distance

39
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:30,560
traveled and the horizontal axis

40
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:33,600
represent time.

41
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:37,680
So at each time T, represented with a

42
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:39,920
point somewhere on this horizontal axis,

43
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:42,560
the height of the graph tells us how far

44
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:44,760
the car has traveled in total after that

45
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:46,640
amount of time.

46
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:48,320
It's pretty common to name a distance

47
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:50,760
function like this S of T.

48
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:52,640
I would use the letter D for distance,

49
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:54,040
but that guy already has another

50
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:56,320
full-time job in calculus.

51
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:58,120
Initially, this curve is quite shallow

52
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,400
since the car is slow to start. During

53
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:02,400
that first second, the distance that it

54
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:05,040
travels doesn't really change that much.

55
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:06,600
Then for the next few seconds, as the

56
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:08,920
car speeds up, the distance traveled in

57
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:10,840
a given second gets larger, which

58
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:12,720
corresponds to a steeper slope in this

59
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:13,959
graph.

60
00:02:13,959 --> 00:02:15,320
And then towards the end, when it slows

61
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:19,040
down, that curve shallows out again.

62
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:22,280
And if we were to plot the car's

63
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:24,640
velocity in meters per second as a

64
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:26,560
function of time, it might look like

65
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:27,840
this bump.

66
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:29,480
At early times, the velocity is very

67
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:31,600
small. Up to the middle of the journey,

68
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:33,240
the car builds up to some maximum

69
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:35,320
velocity, covering a relatively large

70
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:37,720
distance each second.

71
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:39,160
Then it slows back down towards the

72
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:41,680
speed of zero.

73
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:43,120
And these two curves here are definitely

74
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:45,080
related to each other, right? If you

75
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:47,400
change the specific distance versus time

76
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:48,880
function, you're going to have some

77
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:51,640
different velocity versus time function.

78
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:53,360
And what we want to understand is the

79
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:56,120
specifics of that relationship. Exactly

80
00:02:56,120 --> 00:02:58,720
how does velocity depend on a distance

81
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:01,800
versus time function?

82
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:03,120
And to do that, it's worth taking a

83
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:05,239
moment to think critically about what

84
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:08,120
exactly velocity means here.

85
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:09,560
Intuitively, we all might know what

86
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:11,760
velocity at a given moment means. It's

87
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:13,320
just whatever the car's speedometer

88
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:16,560
shows in that moment.

89
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:18,320
And intuitively, it might make sense

90
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,519
that the car's velocity should be higher

91
00:03:20,519 --> 00:03:22,160
at times when this distance function is

92
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:24,239
steeper, when the car traverses more

93
00:03:24,239 --> 00:03:26,560
distance per unit time.

94
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:28,640
But the funny thing is, velocity at a

95
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:31,840
single moment makes no sense. If I show

96
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:34,000
you a picture of a car, just a snapshot

97
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,040
in an instant, and I ask you how fast

98
00:03:36,040 --> 00:03:38,120
it's going, you'd have no way of telling

99
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:39,640
me.

100
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:41,440
What you'd need are two separate points

101
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:43,120
in time to compare.

102
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:44,800
That way you can compute whatever the

103
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:46,600
change in distance across those times

104
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:49,480
is, divided by the change in time.

105
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:51,640
Right? I mean, that's what velocity is.

106
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:53,760
It's the distance traveled per unit

107
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:55,600
time.

108
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,120
So, how is it that we're looking at a

109
00:03:57,120 --> 00:03:59,320
function for velocity that only takes in

110
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:01,760
a single value of T, a single snapshot

111
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:03,080
in time?

112
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:04,760
It's weird, isn't it? We want to

113
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:06,720
associate individual points in time with

114
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:09,000
a velocity, but actually computing

115
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,520
velocity requires comparing two separate

116
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:14,400
points in times.

117
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:17,000
If that feels strange and paradoxical,

118
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,880
good. You're grappling with the same

119
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:20,560
conflicts that the fathers of calculus

120
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:22,440
did. And if you want a deep

121
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:24,320
understanding for rates of change, not

122
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:26,320
just for a moving car, but for all sorts

123
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:28,000
of things in science, you're going to

124
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,960
need to resolve this apparent paradox.

125
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:33,880
First, I think it's best to talk about

126
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:35,600
the real world, and then we'll go into a

127
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,400
purely mathematical one.

128
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:38,720
Let's think about what the car's

129
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:41,120
speedometer is probably doing.

130
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:43,080
At some point, say 3 seconds into the

131
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:45,040
journey, the speedometer might measure

132
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:47,160
how far the car goes in a very small

133
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:49,000
amount of time. Maybe the distance

134
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,840
traveled between 3 seconds and 3.01

135
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:53,320
seconds.

136
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:54,840
Then, it could compute the speed in

137
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:57,120
meters per second as that tiny distance

138
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,680
traversed in meters divided by that tiny

139
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:02,680
time, 0.01 seconds.

140
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:04,800
That is, a physical car just sidesteps

141
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:06,680
the paradox and doesn't actually compute

142
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:08,760
speed at a single point in time. It

143
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:10,720
computes speed during a very small

144
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:13,040
amount of time.

145
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:14,720
So, let's call that difference in time

146
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:16,720
dt, which you might think of in this

147
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:19,280
case as 0.01 seconds. And let's call

148
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:21,520
that resulting difference in distance

149
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:23,280
ds.

150
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:25,120
So, the velocity at some point in time

151
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:28,320
is ds divided by dt, the tiny change in

152
00:05:28,320 --> 00:05:31,360
distance over the tiny change in time.

153
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:33,480
Graphically, you can imagine zooming in

154
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:35,320
on some point of this distance versus

155
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:38,360
time graph above T equals 3.

156
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:41,080
That dt is a small step to the right,

157
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:43,520
since time is on the horizontal axis.

158
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:46,240
And that ds is the resulting change in

159
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:48,160
the height of the graph, since the

160
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:49,960
vertical axis represents the distance

161
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:51,360
traveled.

162
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:53,840
So, ds / dt

163
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:55,120
is something you can think of as the

164
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,800
rise over run slope between two very

165
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:00,560
close points on this graph.

166
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:02,080
Of course, there's nothing special about

167
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:04,880
the value t = 3. We could apply this to

168
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:06,960
any other point in time, so we consider

169
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:09,800
this expression ds/dt to be a function

170
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:12,240
of t. Something where I can give you a

171
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:14,480
time t and you can give me back the

172
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:16,960
value of this ratio at that time, the

173
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:19,760
velocity as a function of time.

174
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:21,640
So, for example, when I had the computer

175
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:23,240
draw this bump curve here, the one

176
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:25,240
representing the velocity function,

177
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:26,840
here's what I had the computer actually

178
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:27,880
do.

179
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:30,680
First, I chose a small value for dt. I

180
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:33,360
think in this case it was 0.01.

181
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:35,240
Then, I had the computer look at a whole

182
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:38,320
bunch of times t between 0 and 10 and

183
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:41,440
compute the distance function s at t +

184
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,800
dt and then subtract off the value of

185
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:45,520
that function at t.

186
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:47,240
In other words, that's the difference in

187
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:49,400
the distance traveled between the given

188
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:53,200
time t and the time 0.01 seconds after

189
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:54,440
that.

190
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:55,680
Then, you can just divide that

191
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:58,440
difference by the change in time, dt,

192
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:00,840
and that gives you velocity in m/s

193
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:04,320
around each point in time.

194
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:06,040
So, with a formula like this, you could

195
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:08,080
give the computer any curve representing

196
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:10,520
any distance function s(t) and it could

197
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:12,280
figure out the curve representing

198
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:13,880
velocity.

199
00:07:13,880 --> 00:07:15,680
So, now would be a good time to pause,

200
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:17,240
reflect, make sure that this idea of

201
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:19,240
relating distance to velocity by looking

202
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:21,760
at tiny changes makes sense. Because

203
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:23,320
what we're going to do is tackle the

204
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:27,200
paradox of the derivative head on.

205
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:30,880
This idea of ds/dt, a tiny change in the

206
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:33,480
value of the function s divided by the

207
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:35,960
tiny change in the input that caused it,

208
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:38,680
that's almost what a derivative is.

209
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:40,720
And even though a car's speedometer will

210
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:42,480
actually look at a concrete change in

211
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:45,400
time like 0.01 seconds. And even though

212
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:46,760
the drawing program here is looking at

213
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:49,200
an actual concrete change in time,

214
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:51,520
in pure math, the derivative is not this

215
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:55,360
ratio ds/dt for a specific choice of dt.

216
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:57,200
Instead, it's whatever that ratio

217
00:07:57,200 --> 00:07:59,760
approaches as your choice for dt

218
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:02,600
approaches zero.

219
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:04,480
Luckily, there is a really nice visual

220
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,280
understanding for what it means to ask

221
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:08,840
what this ratio approaches.

222
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:11,360
Remember, for any specific choice of dt,

223
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:14,120
this ratio ds/dt is the slope of a line

224
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:15,880
passing through two separate points on

225
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:17,720
the graph, right?

226
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:20,800
Well, as dt approaches zero, and as

227
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:23,120
those two points approach each other,

228
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:25,480
the slope of the line approaches the

229
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:27,440
slope of a line that's tangent to the

230
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:29,480
graph at whatever point t we're looking

231
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:30,520
at.

232
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:32,120
So, the true, honest-to-goodness, pure

233
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:34,320
math derivative is not the rise-over-run

234
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:36,120
slope between two nearby points on the

235
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:38,919
graph. It's equal to the slope of a line

236
00:08:38,919 --> 00:08:42,200
tangent to the graph at a single point.

237
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:44,320
Now, notice what I'm not saying. I'm not

238
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:45,920
saying that the derivative is whatever

239
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:48,120
happens when dt is infinitely small,

240
00:08:48,120 --> 00:08:49,920
whatever that would mean.

241
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:51,600
Nor am I saying that you plug in zero

242
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:52,960
for dt.

243
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,120
This dt is always a finitely small,

244
00:08:55,120 --> 00:08:57,320
non-zero value. It's just that it

245
00:08:57,320 --> 00:09:00,800
approaches zero as all.

246
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:05,480
I think that's really clever. Even

247
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:07,080
though change in an instant makes no

248
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:09,880
sense, this idea of letting dt approach

249
00:09:09,880 --> 00:09:12,680
zero is a really sneaky back door way to

250
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:15,080
talk reasonably about the rate of change

251
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:16,920
at a single point in time.

252
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:19,040
Isn't that neat? It's kind of flirting

253
00:09:19,040 --> 00:09:20,960
with the paradox of change in an instant

254
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:22,640
without ever needing to actually touch

255
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:24,640
it. And it comes with such a nice visual

256
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:26,320
intuition, too, as the slope of a

257
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:28,120
tangent line to a single point on the

258
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:30,000
graph.

259
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:31,680
And because change in an instant still

260
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:33,600
makes no sense, I think it's healthiest

261
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:35,240
for you to think of the slope not as

262
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:37,800
some instantaneous rate of change, but

263
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:39,600
instead as the best constant

264
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:41,640
approximation for a rate of change

265
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:44,280
around a point.

266
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:45,640
By the way, it's worth saying a couple

267
00:09:45,640 --> 00:09:47,640
words on notation here. Throughout this

268
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:49,960
video, I've been using dt to refer to a

269
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:52,760
tiny change in t with some actual size

270
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:54,840
and ds to refer to the resulting change

271
00:09:54,840 --> 00:09:58,200
in s, which again has an actual size.

272
00:09:58,200 --> 00:09:59,800
And this is because that's how I want

273
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:01,600
you to think about them.

274
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:03,320
But the convention in calculus is that

275
00:10:03,320 --> 00:10:05,080
whenever you're using the letter d like

276
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:07,040
this, you're kind of announcing your

277
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:08,720
intention that eventually you're going

278
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:10,720
to see what happens as dt approaches

279
00:10:10,720 --> 00:10:11,840
zero.

280
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:13,520
For example, the honest-to-goodness pure

281
00:10:13,520 --> 00:10:16,080
math derivative is written as ds divided

282
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:18,640
by dt, even though it's technically not

283
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:20,800
a fraction per se, but whatever that

284
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:22,640
fraction approaches for smaller and

285
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:25,640
smaller nudges in t.

286
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:27,200
I think a specific example should help

287
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:28,160
here.

288
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:29,760
You might think that asking about what

289
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:31,440
this ratio approaches for smaller and

290
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:33,480
smaller values would make it much more

291
00:10:33,480 --> 00:10:36,080
difficult to compute. But weirdly, it

292
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:38,120
kind of makes things easier.

293
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:39,480
Let's say that you have a given distance

294
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:41,320
versus time function that happens to be

295
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:44,800
exactly t cubed. So, after 1 second, the

296
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:47,600
car has traveled 1 cubed equals 1 m.

297
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:49,880
After 2 seconds, it's traveled 2 cubed

298
00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:52,960
or 8 m and so on.

299
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:54,400
Now, what I'm about to do might seem

300
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:56,480
somewhat complicated, but once the dust

301
00:10:56,480 --> 00:10:58,520
settles, it really is simpler. And more

302
00:10:58,520 --> 00:10:59,760
importantly, it's the kind of thing that

303
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:01,120
you only ever have to do once in

304
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:03,040
calculus.

305
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:04,240
Let's say you wanted to compute the

306
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:07,040
velocity ds divided by dt at some

307
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:09,960
specific time like t equals 2.

308
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:11,680
And for right now, let's think of dt as

309
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:13,839
having an actual size, some concrete

310
00:11:13,839 --> 00:11:15,680
nudge. We'll let it go to zero in just a

311
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:17,000
bit.

312
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,360
The tiny change in distance between 2

313
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:22,560
seconds and 2 plus dt seconds, well,

314
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:26,040
that's s of 2 plus dt minus s of 2.

315
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:28,560
And we divide that by dt.

316
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:30,360
Since our function is t cubed, that

317
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:33,840
numerator looks like 2 + dt cubed - 2

318
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:35,160
cubed.

319
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:37,160
And this This is something we can work

320
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:39,720
out algebraically. Again, bear with me.

321
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:40,960
There's a reason that I'm showing you

322
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:42,600
the details here.

323
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:44,760
When you expand that top, what you get

324
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:49,440
is 2 cubed + 3 * 2 squared dt

325
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:53,000
+ 3 * 2 * dt squared

326
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:58,200
+ dt cubed and all of that is - 2 cubed.

327
00:11:58,200 --> 00:11:59,880
Now, there's a lot of terms, and I want

328
00:11:59,880 --> 00:12:01,320
you to remember that it looks like a

329
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:03,640
mess, but it does simplify.

330
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:06,560
Those 2 cubed terms, they cancel out.

331
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:08,480
And then everything remaining here has a

332
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:10,920
dt in it. And since there's a dt on the

333
00:12:10,920 --> 00:12:12,960
bottom there, many of those cancel out

334
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:14,080
as well.

335
00:12:14,080 --> 00:12:16,640
What this means is that the ratio ds /

336
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:20,920
dt has boiled down into 3 * 2 squared

337
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:23,400
plus Well, two different terms that each

338
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:25,400
have a dt in them.

339
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:27,800
So, if we ask what happens as dt

340
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:29,880
approaches zero, representing the idea

341
00:12:29,880 --> 00:12:31,240
of looking at a smaller and smaller

342
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:33,360
change in time, we can just completely

343
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:36,040
ignore those other terms.

344
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:37,839
By eliminating the need to think about a

345
00:12:37,839 --> 00:12:40,800
specific dt, we've actually eliminated a

346
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:42,480
lot of the complication in the full

347
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:43,839
expression.

348
00:12:43,839 --> 00:12:45,400
So, what we're left with is this nice

349
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:48,320
clean 3 * 2 squared.

350
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:49,640
You can think of that as meaning that

351
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:52,040
the slope of a line tangent to the point

352
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:55,560
at t = 2 of this graph is exactly 3 * 2

353
00:12:55,560 --> 00:12:58,160
squared, or 12.

354
00:12:58,160 --> 00:12:59,600
And of course, there's nothing special

355
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:01,880
about the time t = 2. We could more

356
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:04,280
generally say that the derivative of t

357
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:07,560
cubed as a function of t is 3 * t

358
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:10,120
squared.

359
00:13:11,040 --> 00:13:12,680
Now, take a step back because that's

360
00:13:12,680 --> 00:13:15,200
beautiful. The derivative is this crazy

361
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:17,560
complicated idea. We've got tiny changes

362
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:19,760
in distance over tiny changes in time,

363
00:13:19,760 --> 00:13:21,480
but instead of looking at any specific

364
00:13:21,480 --> 00:13:23,360
one of those, we're talking about what

365
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:26,000
that thing approaches. I mean, that's a

366
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,160
lot to think about. And yet, what we've

367
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:29,480
come out with is such a simple

368
00:13:29,480 --> 00:13:32,840
expression, 3 * t squared.

369
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:34,560
And in practice, you wouldn't go through

370
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:36,760
all this algebra each time. Knowing that

371
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:39,080
the derivative of t cubed is 3t squared

372
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:40,760
is one of those things that all calculus

373
00:13:40,760 --> 00:13:42,600
students learn how to do immediately

374
00:13:42,600 --> 00:13:44,040
without having to re-derive it each

375
00:13:44,040 --> 00:13:46,040
time. And in the next video, I'm going

376
00:13:46,040 --> 00:13:47,400
to show you a nice way to think about

377
00:13:47,400 --> 00:13:48,920
this and a couple other derivative

378
00:13:48,920 --> 00:13:52,720
formulas in really nice geometric ways.

379
00:13:52,720 --> 00:13:54,160
But the point I want to make by showing

380
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:56,280
you all of the algebraic guts here is

381
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:57,800
that when you consider the tiny change

382
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:00,000
in distance caused by a tiny change in

383
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,080
time for some specific value of dt,

384
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:05,520
you'd have kind of a mess. But when you

385
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:07,800
consider what that ratio approaches as

386
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:10,240
dt approaches zero, it lets you ignore

387
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:11,920
much of that mess, and it really does

388
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:13,600
simplify the problem.

389
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:15,160
That right there is kind of the heart of

390
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:18,160
why calculus becomes useful.

391
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:19,720
Another reason to show you a concrete

392
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:21,600
derivative like this is that it sets the

393
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:23,440
stage for an example of the kind of

394
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:25,760
paradoxes that come about if you believe

395
00:14:25,760 --> 00:14:27,120
too much in the illusion of

396
00:14:27,120 --> 00:14:29,760
instantaneous rate of change.

397
00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:31,760
So, think about the actual car traveling

398
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:33,600
according to this t cubed distance

399
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:36,080
function, and consider its motion at the

400
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:38,280
moment t equals zero, right at the

401
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:39,480
start.

402
00:14:39,480 --> 00:14:41,520
Now, ask yourself whether or not the car

403
00:14:41,520 --> 00:14:45,440
is moving at that time.

404
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:47,280
On the one hand, we can compute its

405
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:48,680
speed at that point using the

406
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:51,760
derivative, 3t squared, which for time t

407
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:54,520
equals zero works out to be zero.

408
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:56,440
Visually, this means that the tangent

409
00:14:56,440 --> 00:14:58,360
line to the graph at that point is

410
00:14:58,360 --> 00:15:00,520
perfectly flat. So, the car's {quote}

411
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:04,000
instantaneous velocity is zero. And that

412
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
suggests that obviously it's not moving.

413
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:08,600
But on the other hand, if it doesn't

414
00:15:08,600 --> 00:15:11,200
start moving at time zero, when does it

415
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:12,720
start moving?

416
00:15:12,720 --> 00:15:14,120
Really, pause and ponder that for a

417
00:15:14,120 --> 00:15:16,960
moment. Is the car moving at time t

418
00:15:16,960 --> 00:15:19,839
equals zero?

419
00:15:22,520 --> 00:15:24,200
Do Do see the paradox?

420
00:15:24,200 --> 00:15:25,560
The issue is that the question makes no

421
00:15:25,560 --> 00:15:28,080
sense. It references the idea of change

422
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:29,920
in a moment, but that doesn't actually

423
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:31,640
exist. That's just not what the

424
00:15:31,640 --> 00:15:33,440
derivative measures.

425
00:15:33,440 --> 00:15:35,040
What it means for the derivative of a

426
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:37,400
distance function to be zero is that the

427
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:39,280
best constant approximation for the

428
00:15:39,280 --> 00:15:42,360
car's velocity around that point is zero

429
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:44,000
meters per second.

430
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:45,600
For example, if you look at an actual

431
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:48,080
change in time, say between time zero

432
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:51,560
and 0.1 seconds, the car does move. It

433
00:15:51,560 --> 00:15:54,600
moves 0.001 meters.

434
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:56,600
That's very small, and importantly, it's

435
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:58,480
very small compared to the change in

436
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:00,840
time, giving an average speed of only

437
00:16:00,840 --> 00:16:04,000
0.01 meters per second.

438
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:05,640
And remember, what it means for the

439
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:08,160
derivative of this motion to be zero is

440
00:16:08,160 --> 00:16:10,000
that for smaller and smaller nudges in

441
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,680
time, this ratio of meters per second

442
00:16:12,680 --> 00:16:14,760
approaches zero.

443
00:16:14,760 --> 00:16:16,160
But that's not to say that the car is

444
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:18,720
static. Approximating its movement with

445
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:21,160
a constant velocity of zero is, after

446
00:16:21,160 --> 00:16:24,240
all, just an approximation.

447
00:16:24,240 --> 00:16:25,839
So, whenever you hear people refer to

448
00:16:25,839 --> 00:16:27,920
the derivative as an instantaneous rate

449
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:29,520
of change, a phrase which is

450
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:31,920
intrinsically oxymoronic, I want you to

451
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:34,040
think of that as a conceptual shorthand

452
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:36,800
for the best constant approximation for

453
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:39,040
rate of change.

454
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:40,320
In the next couple of videos, I'll be

455
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:42,000
talking more about the derivative, what

456
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:43,720
it looks like in different contexts, how

457
00:16:43,720 --> 00:16:45,160
do you actually compute it, why is it

458
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:47,160
useful, things like that, focusing on

459
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:50,880
visual intuition, as always.